WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:01.830
- Alright, welcome back!

00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:03.810
I'm here with the one and only, Cher!

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:05.060
Give it up!

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:07.500
(audience cheering happily)

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:08.940
Alright.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:12.960
Well, what is this 1965
photo of you and Sonny

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:14.940
remind you of when you see this?

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:16.710
- Well, we were in the Hilton Hotel,

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:20.495
which we had been kicked
out of about a week before.

00:00:20.495 --> 00:00:21.540
(laughing amusingly)

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:22.770
And we're just hanging out.

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:26.400
We'd done a PLUS Conference
and we're just hanging out.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:29.216
- I love that you got...
What'd you get kicked out for?

00:00:29.216 --> 00:00:30.049
(laughing amusingly)

00:00:30.049 --> 00:00:32.280
- Well because if you
look the way we look,

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:34.680
people were trying to beat us up,

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:37.155
and they just were frightened of it.

00:00:37.155 --> 00:00:37.988
- [Kelly Oh.

00:00:37.988 --> 00:00:39.810
- They didn't understand

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:41.640
that it was nothing to be afraid of,

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:44.905
it was just new or different.

00:00:44.905 --> 00:00:45.990
And that's why we went to England

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:48.480
because we couldn't really
get work in America.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:49.770
- Wow, that's so...

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:51.300
Just, for me and my generation,

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:52.500
I just didn't know all of that.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:54.540
And I obviously, grew up
in a different environment.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:55.740
And so, it's just crazy to me

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:59.190
that you're known for being
such a powerful leader

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:00.023
in the fashion world,

00:01:00.023 --> 00:01:01.770
and being so confident
in that, and being...

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:03.840
And when I mean fashion,
I mean like, real fashion.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.240
Like, I don't really get into a lot of it,

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:07.860
but I love what you do.

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:09.660
I love all those Bob Mackie dresses.

00:01:10.664 --> 00:01:12.150
I love all of that because
it's real true fashion.

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:13.950
It's like, it can be revealing,

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:16.140
but it can also be mysterious,
and it's beautiful,

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:17.160
and it's art.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:19.800
And I think that it's
so crazy for me to think

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:22.080
that back in the day,
people were afraid of that,

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:24.660
but obviously it was a different time.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:26.957
But, let's move on to the 1970s.

00:01:26.957 --> 00:01:28.480
(bright upbeat music)

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:30.630
(audience applauding)

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:34.144
This 70s dress, oh my God, I love it.

00:01:34.144 --> 00:01:35.100
(laughing happily) It's also the image

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:37.410
on the box of your 1970s fragrance.

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:38.243
- [Cher] Right.
(audience cheering happily)

00:01:38.243 --> 00:01:39.213
- So, let's show that.

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:43.050
This is so great.

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:44.910
And it's a Bob Mackie dress, right?

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:45.743
- Oh, yeah.

00:01:45.743 --> 00:01:46.920
That dress has been copied

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:49.296
by every woman I think living now.

00:01:49.296 --> 00:01:50.129
(audience laughing amusingly)

00:01:50.129 --> 00:01:51.990
And they haven't got it right though.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:52.823
- Well, every woman

00:01:52.823 --> 00:01:54.300
that could possibly wear
something like that.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.823
'Cause I think it would look
rather different on me, Cher.

00:01:56.823 --> 00:01:58.320
(audience laughing relatably) I mean...

00:01:58.320 --> 00:01:59.811
- Look how young Bob looks.

00:01:59.811 --> 00:02:00.644
- That's a beautiful...

00:02:00.644 --> 00:02:02.078
- It is a beautiful dress.

00:02:02.078 --> 00:02:02.911
- [Kelly] I know.

00:02:02.911 --> 00:02:06.720
- And it's made out of a material
that is illegal in America

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:09.810
because you know magician's paper,

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:11.100
and it just goes up like that?

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:12.726
Well, so does that dress.

00:02:12.726 --> 00:02:13.559
(audience exclaiming worryingly)

00:02:13.559 --> 00:02:14.478
- So, wait, wait, wait, wait.

00:02:14.478 --> 00:02:15.311
(laughing amusingly)

00:02:15.311 --> 00:02:16.144
So like, if anyone was like...

00:02:16.144 --> 00:02:17.700
A cigarette around you, like it-

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:18.570
- Absolutely.

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:19.620
- Oh my God!

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:21.240
What you do for art and beauty.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:22.950
Oh, that's crazy!

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:24.300
And also, very rock and roll.

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:26.850
And of course, it was like
flammable, you're so awesome.

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:28.440
That's literally one of my favorite things

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:30.270
that you've ever worn, it's so cool.

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:31.633
- No, it's my favorite.

00:02:31.633 --> 00:02:32.760
I have another one though.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:33.593
- Oh, is it?

00:02:33.593 --> 00:02:34.800
What's your favorite?

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:36.660
- Well, that's my favorite.

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:38.730
But, one of my other favorites

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:40.980
is the one with the big giant feathers,

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:43.562
the black one with the big giant feathers.

00:02:43.562 --> 00:02:44.395
(audience applauding happily)

00:02:44.395 --> 00:02:45.726
- Wait, is this...

00:02:45.726 --> 00:02:48.571
Okay, I'm gonna get to
that one in a minute

00:02:48.571 --> 00:02:49.492
'cause I love that one as well.

00:02:49.492 --> 00:02:51.030
(audience cheering happily)

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:52.080
But, is it...

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:53.550
'Cause I'm gonna get to
that one, that one's cool.

00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:56.100
You have so many cool
ones, it's ridiculous.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:58.710
But, I have to ask this 'cause
I remembered hearing this

00:02:58.710 --> 00:02:59.940
and I was like, "I gotta
ask her in an interview,"

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:01.650
is it sure that Jennifer Aniston

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:04.110
would come over to play at
your house as a little girl

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:04.943
in the 70s?

00:03:04.943 --> 00:03:07.560
- As a teenager, I mean as
like, a middle teenager.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:09.705
And eat me outta house and home.

00:03:09.705 --> 00:03:10.957
(audience laughing amusingly)

00:03:10.957 --> 00:03:14.370
And it's like the kids, the
girls always used to, you know,

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:16.383
they knew when I would went to Balducci,

00:03:17.246 --> 00:03:20.010
and they would just show
up and just chow down.

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:22.372
And she was a big chow downer.

00:03:22.372 --> 00:03:23.934
(audience laughing amusingly)

00:03:23.934 --> 00:03:24.960
- I love it!

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:25.793
I didn't know.

00:03:25.793 --> 00:03:26.626
I just think that's so funny.

00:03:26.626 --> 00:03:27.930
Nobody knows that behind the scenes

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:30.120
of like how people that you don't know

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:32.460
are related in some way, in some capacity,

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:33.293
like back in the day.

00:03:33.293 --> 00:03:34.293
That's so cool to me.

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:37.290
Did anybody know that she was acting

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:38.730
and gonna do anything
like that at that age?

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:39.600
'Cause she was so young.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:41.940
- No, I mean she was
going to school, you know,

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:44.220
she was going to High
School of Performing Arts.

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:47.040
And this is one thing that I know,

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:52.040
it's like when my mom used
to say to anybody she met,

00:03:52.597 --> 00:03:54.330
"I have a daughter who sings."

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:57.808
Well, the minute you say
that, people just turn it off.

00:03:57.808 --> 00:03:59.280
And Ahmet Ertegun,

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:03.960
the big manager and owner
of Atlantic Records,

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:05.790
my mom said, "I have
a daughter who sings,"

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:07.530
and he said, "Yes, Georgia, that's good."

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:11.430
And then, he signed us
and then we're singing.

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:13.387
And one night, my mom
comes in and he said,

00:04:13.387 --> 00:04:14.670
"Georgia, what are you doing here?"

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:17.460
And she said, "I told you I
have a daughter that sings,

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:19.214
there she is."

00:04:19.214 --> 00:04:20.250
(audience laughing amusingly)

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:21.450
- Oh my God!

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:22.950
That's so true though because I mean,

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:24.810
you've had to have that
happen so many times too.

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:26.134
I do too.

00:04:26.134 --> 00:04:26.967
They're always like,
"Oh, my daughter sings,"

00:04:26.967 --> 00:04:27.800
or, "Oh, my son sings."

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:30.540
And they always like, think
there's some kind of magical way

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:31.710
I can make it happen for them.

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:32.730
Does that happen to you a lot of times?

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:34.500
'Cause I'm like, "Look,
it's just sometimes,

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:36.690
you're there in a studio, and
somebody's car breaks down,

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:38.850
and you're singing the BGV,
and you get in that way,"

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:40.170
You know, like, "You do it, darlin'."

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:43.230
You just never know how it's gonna happen.

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:44.670
So, don't you get that a lot though?

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:46.770
People saying all the
time, like, "My kid sings"?

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:48.000
- Yeah.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:48.870
And sometimes-

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:50.293
- [Kelly] I know, I get that too.

00:04:50.293 --> 00:04:52.290
- Sometimes, you see them
later, you know, on TV,

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:54.780
and sometimes you can do something,

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:56.340
but most times you can't.

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:57.510
- I know, that's what I'm saying.

00:04:57.510 --> 00:04:58.657
It's always a hard thing to be like,

00:04:58.657 --> 00:05:00.270
"Man, you just gotta
say yes to everything,

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:02.087
and get out there and do it

00:05:02.087 --> 00:05:04.170
'cause you never know
what your chance will be,"

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:05.555
or whatever.
- Right.

00:05:05.555 --> 00:05:07.386
- But now, we're heading into the 1980s.

00:05:07.386 --> 00:05:08.530
(bright upbeat music returns)

00:05:08.530 --> 00:05:10.387
(audience applauding happily)

00:05:10.387 --> 00:05:11.340
"Turn Back Time"

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:14.010
is also the slogan from
your fragrance collection,

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:16.510
which is just so clever.

00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:17.343
(audience cheering supportively)

00:05:17.343 --> 00:05:21.030
But, what do you remember
about that song and that video?

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:23.370
'Cause I feel like sometimes artists,

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:25.740
people don't know the
stories that go behind songs,

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:27.150
when we like end up saying yes

00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:27.990
and we end up recording them.

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:29.070
So, what happened with that song,

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:30.450
how'd that song come to you?

00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:32.580
- Diane Warren wrote it.

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:35.853
And she brought it to me
and I didn't wanna do it.

00:05:35.853 --> 00:05:36.686
(audience laughing gently)

00:05:36.686 --> 00:05:38.970
No, she was doing the demo,

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:43.200
and she is not exactly a singer
that you wanna listen to.

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:46.120
And so I kept saying no.
(audience laughing gently)

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:47.235
(laughing amusingly)

00:05:47.235 --> 00:05:49.530
I kept saying, no, no, and no.

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:50.973
And then, she said,

00:05:52.297 --> 00:05:56.160
"I'll pay for it if it
doesn't turn out great."

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:57.320
Well,

00:05:57.320 --> 00:05:59.917
knowing Diane is the cheapest
woman that ever drew breath-

00:05:59.917 --> 00:06:00.750
(audience laughing gently)

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:03.420
I said, "Okay, I gotta do this
'cause I just gotta do it."

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:07.380
And the moment I started,
the song just sung itself,

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:08.213
do you know what I mean?

00:06:08.213 --> 00:06:09.502
I opened my mouth

00:06:09.502 --> 00:06:11.310
and the song just came
out and sung itself.

00:06:11.310 --> 00:06:13.140
- I'll tell you what, man,
she's one hell of a writer,

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:15.420
but she's also very bold.

00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:16.467
Like, when she sends a song.
- Oh, yes.

00:06:16.467 --> 00:06:19.412
- She was like, "You are
stupid if you don't do this."

00:06:19.412 --> 00:06:21.437
(laughing amusingly)

00:06:21.437 --> 00:06:22.270
You're like, "Okay, okay."

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:24.840
- Babe, she followed me into an AA meeting

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:26.181
to sell me a song.

00:06:26.181 --> 00:06:27.794
(audience laughing amusingly)

00:06:27.794 --> 00:06:28.786
(laughing loudly)

00:06:28.786 --> 00:06:30.570
No sorry, no, it was an Al-Anon meeting.

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:34.050
It was an Al-Anon
meeting, and she came in,

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:36.810
and somebody said, "You
know, are you here,"

00:06:36.810 --> 00:06:37.643
you know, blah-blah-blah.

00:06:37.643 --> 00:06:39.270
She said, "No, I just wanna see Cher

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:41.340
'cause I have a song I want her to hear."

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:43.229
And they kicked her out.

00:06:43.229 --> 00:06:44.062
(audience laughing amusingly)

00:06:44.062 --> 00:06:44.895
- I know, but you know what,

00:06:44.895 --> 00:06:47.280
there's one thing I'll give
that woman, she is persistent.

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:50.073
But, she writes a lotta
great songs, so there's that.

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:52.980
So, what do you remember about that video,

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:53.813
making that video?

00:06:53.813 --> 00:06:55.050
It's so iconic.

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:56.437
- Well, I remember Bob saying,

00:06:56.437 --> 00:06:58.437
"Don't tell anybody I made this."

00:06:59.866 --> 00:07:00.699
(Kelly laughing amusingly)

00:07:00.699 --> 00:07:01.927
And when I see it, I think,

00:07:01.927 --> 00:07:06.060
"You might have gone a little
bit too far this time."

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:07.020
But, whatever.

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:09.300
And so, I just...

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:12.330
Also, we did so many different things.

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:14.310
I went around in a speedboat,

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:16.590
and then I climbed up the side of the,

00:07:16.590 --> 00:07:19.977
you know, of the Missouri, and came...

00:07:19.977 --> 00:07:20.810
- [Kelly] Yes.

00:07:20.810 --> 00:07:23.130
- I mean, we did everything,
all they used was that.

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:24.420
All they used was that.
(audience laughing amusingly)

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:26.070
And my son's in it.

00:07:26.070 --> 00:07:29.220
And it's just, look, it's bold.

00:07:29.220 --> 00:07:34.220
It might be past bold, it's
crass, but I don't care.

00:07:34.337 --> 00:07:35.515
(audience applauding and
cheering supportively)

00:07:35.515 --> 00:07:36.348
- I gotta be honest with you,

00:07:36.348 --> 00:07:39.090
like I actually really dug it.

00:07:39.090 --> 00:07:41.400
I thought it was awesome, and
an incredible domino effect

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:43.410
I think in a positive
way for a lotta people.

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:44.243
I thought it was rad.

00:07:44.243 --> 00:07:45.300
Now, I can't wear that (beep),

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:48.482
but like you can and it
looks good. (laughing warmly)

00:07:48.482 --> 00:07:49.315
(audience applauding supportively)

00:07:49.315 --> 00:07:51.300
Not everybody's blessed.

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:52.620
- No, no.

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:57.000
But you know, it was, for me,

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:01.170
it's never been a statement of
how far I can push something.

00:08:01.170 --> 00:08:03.037
It's always been a statement of,

00:08:03.037 --> 00:08:05.190
"This is what I want to do."

00:08:05.190 --> 00:08:07.440
And let me tell you, you
know, the dress that we saw,

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:09.280
the one that blows up?

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:10.113
(audience laughing amusingly)

00:08:10.113 --> 00:08:12.540
That was on the cover of Time Magazine.

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:15.390
In the South, they ripped the cover off.

00:08:15.390 --> 00:08:17.087
So, you know how far-

00:08:17.087 --> 00:08:17.952
- [Kelly] Oh my G...

00:08:17.952 --> 00:08:18.830
Well, that's not shocking.

00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:19.663
- Yeah. (laughing understandably)

00:08:19.663 --> 00:08:20.496
(audience laughing amusingly)

00:08:20.496 --> 00:08:21.490
- Yeah.

00:08:21.490 --> 00:08:23.010
(audience cheering supportively)

00:08:23.010 --> 00:08:26.070
You had two iconic looks this decade

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:26.903
that we're talking about.

00:08:26.903 --> 00:08:31.248
So, you already talked
about this one from 1986.

00:08:31.248 --> 00:08:32.610
So, this is another one you loved, right?

00:08:32.610 --> 00:08:33.443
- Yes.

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:37.110
Bob designed that for
me, but I had the idea.

00:08:37.110 --> 00:08:42.110
And I asked him to do it
because the academy said

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:44.280
that (laughing gently) the men I chose

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:47.910
were not appropriate and that
my clothing was inappropriate.

00:08:47.910 --> 00:08:49.650
So, I thought, "You think that's bad?

00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:51.078
Wait till you see this."

00:08:51.078 --> 00:08:52.519
(audience laughing relatably)

00:08:52.519 --> 00:08:54.900
- I love that, I love
that. (laughing amusingly)

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:55.773
I love that.

00:08:56.790 --> 00:08:59.024
- Wait, wait, I'm gonna
tell you something,

00:08:59.024 --> 00:08:59.857
I'm gonna tell you something!

00:08:59.857 --> 00:09:01.923
So, I was presenting something, right?

00:09:03.568 --> 00:09:05.070
And, oh no, I was going to present.

00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:07.980
And I was going around the back
and it's all black duvetyne,

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:10.920
which is like black velvet
and you can't see anything.

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:12.120
But when you're back there,

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:13.710
and if you've been in front of the lights,

00:09:13.710 --> 00:09:14.700
you can't see anything.

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:16.470
So, I'm going around, I'm going around,

00:09:16.470 --> 00:09:17.987
and I run into Jane Fonda.

00:09:17.987 --> 00:09:19.417
(laughing amusingly) And she
looks at me and she goes,

00:09:19.417 --> 00:09:22.500
"I have got to get to my seat
before you get on stage, okay?

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:24.149
I just have to see this."

00:09:24.149 --> 00:09:25.267
(audience laughing amusingly)

00:09:25.267 --> 00:09:26.100
So...

00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:28.063
(audience applauding enjoyably)

00:09:28.063 --> 00:09:29.670
- Well, I love that moment,

00:09:29.670 --> 00:09:31.860
and it was very cool for a lot of us.

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:34.416
And finally though, I
wanna talk about the 90s.

00:09:34.416 --> 00:09:35.810
(bright upbeat music returns)

00:09:35.810 --> 00:09:39.462
(audience applauding happily)

00:09:39.462 --> 00:09:40.680
So, this is like the
time I like grew up in,

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:42.150
that I love music and
all that kinda stuff.

00:09:42.150 --> 00:09:44.640
So, how did the song
"Believe" come to you?

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:46.140
'Cause it's such an iconic song.

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:49.920
- Okay, first I got dropped
by my record company.

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:53.317
And a man in England who
ran Warner's UK said,

00:09:53.317 --> 00:09:56.580
"I believe in you," and, "Come
to me and I will help you."

00:09:56.580 --> 00:09:58.110
So, I got "Believe,"

00:09:58.110 --> 00:10:03.110
and the chorus was great
but the verse was terrible.

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:08.700
And so, my producer, who I'd
never had a cross word with,

00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:10.380
kept saying, "You have to sing it better,

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:11.933
you have to sing it better."

00:10:11.933 --> 00:10:12.766
(laughing amusingly) And I said,

00:10:12.766 --> 00:10:14.760
"Babe, if you want it sung
better, get somebody else."

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:16.680
So then, I left.

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:19.027
And then, the next day he said,

00:10:19.027 --> 00:10:22.200
"You know, I've been playing
around with the pitch machine

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:24.540
and I think I might have something."

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:27.480
So, I went in and I sat next to him,

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:29.070
and he started to play it.

00:10:29.070 --> 00:10:30.360
And it was unbelievable

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:32.460
because there was never anything like it.

00:10:32.460 --> 00:10:35.280
And when it was done, we
high fived each other.

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:38.640
And then, the people from the
record company who I love,

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:40.800
and the Germans, I don't
know why they were in there,

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:42.120
but anyway, they were.

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:43.413
Oh, I can't say this.

00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:48.867
So, they said, "Well, no,
we don't know it's you."

00:10:48.867 --> 00:10:52.470
And I went, (laughing
happily) "Exactly, exactly."

00:10:52.470 --> 00:10:54.510
In the beginning and
it spiced up the verse,

00:10:54.510 --> 00:10:56.760
and it just sailed, you know?

00:10:56.760 --> 00:11:00.450
And then, they said, "You can't have it.

00:11:00.450 --> 00:11:03.630
And I said.... Well, can you bleep this?

00:11:03.630 --> 00:11:04.500
- Yeah.
- Okay.

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:05.680
- It's my show, we can
do whatever you want.

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:07.537
- Okay, so anyway, I said,

00:11:07.537 --> 00:11:12.135
"Okay, you can take it out
over my dead (beep) body."

00:11:12.135 --> 00:11:13.807
(audience cheering loudly)

00:11:13.807 --> 00:11:16.080
(laughing amusingly)

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:17.160
- But, thank you so much

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:18.900
for taking us through those decades.

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:21.660
This is such a cool, rad perfume line.

00:11:21.660 --> 00:11:22.980
I love it so much.

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:26.424
I love the whole backstory of it all.

00:11:26.424 --> 00:11:27.780
(laughing happily) But everybody,

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:31.230
Cher is celebrating each
decade of her iconic career

00:11:31.230 --> 00:11:33.840
with a unique fragrance
that conveys the feelings

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:35.970
of the 60s, 70s, 80s, and 90s,

00:11:35.970 --> 00:11:38.940
like we were just talking about,
with four timeless scents.

00:11:38.940 --> 00:11:40.852
See what we did there?

00:11:40.852 --> 00:11:42.960
And everyone here will
get to enjoy those scents

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:45.420
because you're all
going home with a bottle

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:47.313
from "Cher Decades" eau de parfum!

00:11:48.153 --> 00:11:49.016
(Kelly imitating horn blowing)

00:11:49.016 --> 00:11:49.849
(audience cheering ecstatically)

